"comparison": "Both GS and BAC disclose the composition of their CET1 capital regulatory minimums, which include a base minimum, a G-SIB surcharge, and a Stress Capital Buffer (SCB). However, the specific buffer values differ between the two firms for the same period. GS reports an SCB of 3.2% (noted in summary as 6.2% for Standardized, but the peer text clarifies the breakdown) and a G-SIB surcharge of 3.0%, whereas BAC reports an SCB of 3.2% and a G-SIB surcharge of 3.0% for December 31, 2024. Wait, looking closely at GS text: 'SCB of 6.2% as of December 2024... under the Standardized'. Looking at BAC text: 'SCB... of 3.2 percent at December 31, 2024'. There is a discrepancy in the reported SCB values between the two disclosures for the same date, which is a meaningful difference in regulatory capital requirements. Both confirm a 3.0% G-SIB surcharge and 0% countercyclical buffer.",
